### Editing roles
The **Roles** page (`/admin/roles`) edits the matrix at runtime, with no deployment: tick a box to grant a permission, clear it to revoke it, create a role, delete one. Every change goes through the `roles` tRPC router (`packages/api/src/router/roles.ts`: `matrix`, `create`, `setPermission`, `remove`), which calls the rules of `packages/auth/src/role-management.ts`.
- **Only the literal `admin` role manages roles**, through `adminProcedure`, never through a permission a role could be granted: a role that could edit the matrix could give itself everything, so there is no subset check to get wrong. `user,admin`, `Admin` and a role that holds every business permission are refused like any other role.
- **A grant names the code vocabulary.** `setPermission` takes `resource.action` and accepts a name of `businessStatement` only; `user.delete`, `session.revoke` and an unknown name are a 400 and write nothing. Granting a permission a role already holds, or revoking one it does not, changes nothing.
- **`admin` is neither edited nor deleted**, and `admin`, the default role (`DEFAULT_ROLE`, the role every new account starts on) and every key every object has (`constructor`) are reserved: no role is created under any of these ids. The plugin looks a role up by plain object key, so an account on `constructor` would get a 500 from its endpoints; no path accepts the id, a role assigned or created. The default role can be edited, but not deleted, because accounts are created on it with no check that it exists.
- **A role id** matches `^[a-z0-9-]+$` and has at most 40 characters, its label 1 to 64. A duplicate id is refused (the primary key decides, so two requests for one id make one role). There is no rename of an id: it is stored on each account, and renaming it would leave every holder on a role that no longer exists. Create the new role, give its holders the new role, delete the old one.
{{#if (has "database" "d1")}}
- **A held role cannot be deleted, in the write itself.** D1 has no interactive transaction, so a read ("does anyone hold it?") followed by a delete would let an account be given the role in between. `deleteRoleUnlessHeld` (`packages/auth/src/role-store.ts`) is one conditional statement, `DELETE FROM roles WHERE id = ? AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE role = ?)`, sent with its permission rows in one `db.batch` (D1 runs a batch as one transaction; the rows go only once the role is gone, so a refused delete keeps them). Assigning a role is the mirror (`assignRole` writes only while the role has its row), so the two cannot interleave into an account that holds a deleted role. A banned account still holds its role. The grant is conditional the same way, so a role deleted a moment earlier gets no permission row that a role created later under the same id would inherit.
{{else}}
- **A held role cannot be deleted, in one transaction.** PostgreSQL runs each statement of a transaction on the data committed when it starts, so a single `DELETE ... WHERE NOT EXISTS (holders)` would not see an account given the role while it waited. `deleteRoleUnlessHeld` (`packages/auth/src/role-store.ts`) locks the row of the role for update, then counts the holders in a statement of its own, then deletes (the permission rows follow through the foreign key). Assigning a role is the mirror: `assignRole` locks the row of the role for share before it writes, so one of the two waits for the other and the loser sees the winner: an account is never left on a deleted role. A banned account still holds its role. The grant locks the row for share too, so a role deleted a moment earlier gets no permission row that a role created later under the same id would inherit.
{{/if}}
- **A change applies on the next request.** Nothing keeps a permission list between requests (see above), so a revoked box is refused for everyone who holds the role on their next call, and the page does not guess: a box changes only after the server confirmed it and the matrix was fetched again. A box that is saving is `aria-busy` and ignores clicks (it is never `disabled`, which would drop the keyboard focus), and a polite status region says `Saving`, `Saved` or `Not saved: <reason>` and when a click was ignored, so a screen reader hears the result.
- **Refusals are worded as answers** (`No role has that id`, `A role with that id already exists`), never with `does not exist`, `table` or `column`: Better Auth logs a thrown message with one of those at error level, as a database failure.
